---
import DocLayout from '../../components/DocLayout.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/url';
---

<DocLayout
  title="Getting started"
  description="Install SAARTHI-STUDIO, connect your first model, open a project, and run your first chat and agent task."
>
  <p>
    SAARTHI-STUDIO is a local-first AI development workspace: one Rust
    service owns your conversations, files, terminals, agents, and model
    traffic, and the UI is the same whether it runs in the native window
    or a browser. This page takes you from a download to a first reviewed
    agent change.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Install for your platform</h2>
  <p>
    Grab the current release from the <a href={withBase('/download/')}>downloads section</a> —
    there is a dedicated page with install steps for
    <a href={withBase('/download/linux/')}>Linux</a>,
    <a href={withBase('/download/windows/')}>Windows</a>,
    <a href={withBase('/download/macos/')}>macOS</a>,
    <a href={withBase('/download/android/')}>Android</a>, and
    <a href={withBase('/download/vscode/')}>VS Code</a>.
    Verify the SHA-256 against the release's <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code> before installing.
  </p>
  <p>
    On first launch the app opens its own window and starts the local
    service at <code>http://127.0.0.1:8765</code>. Prefer a browser tab?
    Run <code>SAARTHI-STUDIO --browser</code>. Want a headless server?
    <code>SAARTHI-STUDIO --no-gui</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Connect a model</h2>
  <p>
    Studio talks to OpenAI-compatible endpoints. Two common paths:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A router.</strong> Point Settings at FreeLLMAPI or another
      compatible router and paste its unified API key. The router owns
      provider credentials, fallback chains, and budgets — Studio never
      stores upstream provider keys.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A local model.</strong> Run Ollama on the same machine and
      pick a model in the Local AI panel. Nothing leaves the computer.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Use the connection test in Settings to confirm the round trip before
    your first message. Details live in
    <a href={withBase('/docs/providers/')}>Providers &amp; models</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Open a project</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>Open folder…</strong> and pick the codebase you want to
    work on. Everything workspace-related is confined to that folder:
    the file tree, the editor, search, the terminal's working directory,
    and Git operations. You can keep several projects and switch between
    them; threads are scoped to the active project.
  </p>

  <h2>4. First chat</h2>
  <p>
    Ask a question in the composer. Attach context with the
    <strong>Context</strong> chip (open files, selections), attach a skill
    with the <strong>Skill</strong> chip, or toggle <strong>Deep</strong>
    for cited web research. Replies stream in with a working Stop button,
    and the token meter shows how much of the request budget you are
    using.
  </p>

  <h2>5. First agent run</h2>
  <p>
    Switch the composer to <strong>Build</strong> mode and describe a
    change. The agent plans steps, and privileged actions — file writes
    and commands — pause for your approval. Inspect the step log and
    diffs, then keep, restore, or apply the result. Writing runs can work
    in an isolated Git worktree so your checkout stays untouched until
    you decide. The full model is in
    <a href={withBase('/docs/agents/')}>Agents &amp; runs</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={withBase('/docs/workspace/')}>Workspace &amp; IDE</a> — editor, terminal, Git, diagnostics, debugging.</li>
    <li><a href={withBase('/docs/skills-and-mcp/')}>Skills &amp; MCP</a> — extend what the assistant knows and can call.</li>
    <li><a href={withBase('/docs/privacy/')}>Privacy &amp; security</a> — exactly what stays local and what leaves.</li>
    <li><a href={withBase('/docs/troubleshooting/')}>Troubleshooting</a> — when something does not start.</li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
